At Samsung Research, I worked on a practical obstacle to personalizing language models on mobile devices: fine-tuning a 1 GB model exceeded the device’s available memory, while CPU execution was too slow. Addressing these constraints required changes across the learning algorithm and the execution system, from how model state was stored to how work was scheduled between the CPU and NPU. This experience shaped my interest in efficient machine learning: understanding which representations and computations a task needs, and designing systems that execute them effectively. At UC San Diego, I hope to strengthen my foundations in machine learning and computer systems and apply them to efficient multimodal models and distributed inference.

My interest in this question began at IIIT Hyderabad’s Speech Processing Lab, where I worked with Prof. Anil Kumar Vuppala on whispered-to-normal speech conversion. Whispered speech lacks the vocal-fold excitation present in normal speech, so a model must infer missing information from the remaining spectral cues. I explored richer spectral representations within HuBERT’s self-supervised pretraining to improve reconstruction under whispered and noisy conditions. Their higher dimensionality, however, increased computational cost. By applying spectral smoothing and principal-component selection, I learned to balance the information captured by a representation against the resources needed to use it.

In my undergraduate Honours research, I encountered a similar trade-off within the model itself. To distinguish typical disfluencies from atypical stuttering, I compared representations from different layers of self-supervised speech encoders. HuBERT’s fifth layer, out of 24, achieved 97% F1 across disfluency categories and outperformed the final-layer representation. I also contributed to the IIITH-TISA stuttered-speech corpus and co-authored the resulting paper, published at Interspeech 2025. The finding showed me that a task does not always require the full depth of a model, prompting me to think about how computation could adapt to the information needed for a particular input.

I wanted to understand how these efficiency questions translate to hardware, so I joined Samsung Research as an intern working on SnapLite, an on-device runtime serving over 200 production models. Deploying a model across CPUs, GPUs, and NPUs required separate conversion and validation for vendor-specific runtimes. I built a unified deployment and verification pipeline that preserved processor-specific kernel optimizations, and added graph partitioning to support concurrent execution across two processors. The pipeline reduced time to first inference tenfold and was adopted by multiple product teams after I presented it at Samsung Research’s annual conference. The project introduced me to the importance of compilation, placement, and scheduling in determining how efficiently a model runs.

After returning to Samsung Research full-time, I worked with Samsung Research Korea on NNTrainer, an open-source generative-AI engine, through a project on privacy-preserving on-device language model personalization. Fine-tuning a 1 GB model peaked at 3 GB of memory because the device had to hold model weights and retained activations. I combined 4-bit base weights with LoRA adapters trained using quantization-aware training, then introduced weight checkpointing and selective activation recomputation. Together, these changes reduced peak memory use to under 0.5 GB.

Once memory was no longer the primary constraint, CPU throughput became the bottleneck. I moved compute-intensive operations to the NPU, but synchronization and data transfers left its matrix engines underutilized. To address this, I designed an asynchronous dispatch queue with zero-copy shared memory, allowing data movement to overlap with computation. Training became eight times faster than CPU execution, and the CPU–NPU execution design has been filed for a patent. Working through these successive bottlenecks taught me to consider learning algorithms and execution systems together: an algorithmic improvement can expose a systems constraint, and vice versa.

That experience led me to investigate another approach to on-device training. NNTrainer supported NPU backpropagation, while GGML’s Llama runtime offered a faster precompiled forward path but lacked backward support. I explored zeroth-order optimization, which estimates updates using forward passes alone, to make adaptation possible on that runtime. Full-weight perturbations exceeded device memory and dispatch limits, so I restricted updates to a gradient-informed low-rank subspace and used orthogonal projections to retain knowledge across tasks. On a mobile NPU, the method matched or exceeded standard LoRA in accuracy and retention and completed 1,000 steps in 20 minutes—five times faster than prior on-device zeroth-order training. This work is under review at MLSys 2027. I am now developing a hybrid optimizer in which the device performs local zeroth-order updates while a server periodically provides first-order guidance. One question I am investigating is how often that guidance is needed before stale update directions affect convergence.

As I worked within the limits of a single device, I also became interested in how coordinating computation with a server could make inference more efficient. When an on-device model escalates a request, a server may need to process the conversation context again. I adapted speculative decoding so that the device continues drafting candidate tokens while the server verifies them in a parallel pass. In early prototype evaluations against full-context server recomputation, the approach reduced end-to-end latency by 1.4 times and server computation by 2.8 times. I am also exploring early exits in the on-device drafter and block generation with discrete diffusion models to reduce sequential computation before verification. Together, these projects have made me interested in when computation should be performed, reused, or moved across devices.

I have begun exploring the same question in server-side vision-language model reinforcement learning. Multiple rollouts for a high-resolution image can request overlapping crops, creating opportunities to share visual processing. After reusing initial image representations across queries, I began investigating whether crop requests can also be batched across rollouts and overlapping processing avoided. This introduces a resource-allocation problem: shared visual processing must be coordinated with the growing memory requirements of individual rollouts. My experience so far has helped me identify these opportunities, but I want a deeper understanding of distributed systems, optimization, and machine learning to reason about their trade-offs beyond the settings I have worked with directly.

The M.S. in Computer Science at UC San Diego would give me the opportunity to build that foundation through advanced coursework and apply it through thesis research. I am particularly interested in studying how multimodal systems can decide which representations to retain, compress, or recompute under memory constraints. Prof. Zhijian Liu’s work on NVILA connects to this interest in reducing visual-token redundancy and to my earlier work on selecting useful intermediate representations. I am also drawn to Prof. Hao Zhang’s work on distributed training and disaggregated LLM serving, including DistServe. It would provide a valuable setting in which to extend what I have learned about device–server coordination to scheduling and state management across larger systems. Through coursework and research, I hope to develop the breadth to evaluate both the model-level and systems-level consequences of these choices.

Alongside research, I led six undergraduates in developing Waveform-Wizard, an open-source Python platform that replaced our lab’s proprietary MATLAB speech-analysis workflows and was presented at ICASSP 2025. I also served as an undergraduate teaching assistant for Real Analysis and Linear Algebra. These experiences showed me how much I value helping others build confidence with difficult technical ideas, and I hope to continue mentoring peers through the IDEA Engineering Student Center.

At UC San Diego, I hope to bring my experience building efficient ML systems while gaining the theoretical and systems foundations to approach larger and more varied problems. My goal is to become a research engineer who designs scalable, hardware-aware ML systems and makes capable models practical across a range of computational environments.
